@NODE "intro" "MorphUAE - Introduction"
@PREV "main"
@NEXT "req"

 @{b}@{u}Introduction@{uu}@{ub}
 
 MorphUAE is an enhanced variant of the emulator E-UAE specially made for MorphOS.

 You might think to yourself, isn't there already a port of E-UAE for MorphOS ?
 ... and the answer to that is of course yes! However, the version ported done 
 by Fabien Coeurjoly was last updated more than 10 years ago.

 E-UAE is very Un*x-like in it's configuration handling which only consists of 
 textfiles. It also lacks MUI-support, so it's purely an Intuition window.

 So, that was the key idea of the birth of MorphUAE. 
 Add an improved and easier user experience with some extra new features.

@ENDNODE

@NODE "req" "MorphUAE - Requirements"
@PREV "intro"
@NEXT "install"

 @{b}@{u}Requirements@{uu}@{ub}

 This program needs the following to run :

 · MorphOS 3.x or higher
 · TrueColour / 24Bit graphics
 · Amiga Kickstart ROMs (for best compatibility)

@ENDNODE

@NODE "install" "MorphUAE - Installation"
@PREV "req"
@NEXT "usage"

 @{b}@{u}Installation@{uu}@{ub}

 This program doesn't need any installation, so because of that it doesn't come
 with any form of installer script. Just place it where you want and run it 
 from there! =)

 Place your Amiga Kickstart files in the folder named @{b}Kickstarts@{ub}
 The default name which the emulator uses for the name here is:-

 @{b}Kick1.rom@{ub} : OCS based hardware
 @{b}Kick2.rom@{ub} : ECS based hardware
 @{b}Kick3.rom@{ub} : AGA based hardware
 @{b}rom.key@{ub}   : Your AmigaForever/Cloanto keyfile 
 . . . . . . . . . . . (if you have encrypted ROM files that is)

 However, the name can be anything and can be modified in the settings window 
 for each machine type. Naming the files as in the list above should get you a 
 running emulator directly, without the need for any changes.

 MorphUAE comes bundled with AROS m68k ROM images which is used in case there 
 are no real Amiga Kickstarts found. This is a re-implementaion of the official
 Kickstart for m68k Amigas. It's not perfect but should work "well enough" in 
 most cases. For best compatibility it's always best to try to use a real Amiga
 Kickstart ROM.

@ENDNODE

@NODE "usage" "MorphUAE - Usage"
@PREV "install"
@NEXT "faq"

 @{b}@{u}Usage@{uu}@{ub}

 This program's user interface consists of a couple of fields/elements,
 which are briefly explained below...

  @{" Main Window  " LINK "usage-main"     }
  @{" Settings     " LINK "usage-settings" }
  @{" CLI / Shell  " LINK "usage-shell"    }
  @{" Tooltypes    " LINK "usage-tooltype" }
  @{" Ambient      " LINK "usage-ambient"  }
  @{" Hotkeys      " LINK "usage-hotkeys"  }
  @{" ARexx        " LINK "usage-arexx"    }

@ENDNODE

@NODE "usage-main" "MorphUAE - Main Window"
@PREV "usage"
@NEXT "usage-settings"

 @{b}· Main window :@{ub}

   The first thing you see when you start the emulator is the main window.
   This window consists mainly of a render area for the emulated graphics and 
   below that you have a toolbar.


 @{b}· Toolbar :@{ub}
   
   From this toolbar you can change the emulator settings and control its 
   behaviour. The toolbar consists of a number of clickable symbols that are 
   described below.


    @{b}· Settings :@{ub}

      This button will open the settings window so you can change your various 
      setups easily.


    @{b}· Reset :@{ub}

      This button will reset the emulator in either a Soft or Hard reset
      (depending on what you select in the settings window on the General tab).
      When pressing this button you will be presented with an popup menu where
      you can select the normal reset or you can also reset and change to 
      a specific machine type.


    @{b}· Fullscreen :@{ub}

      By pressing this button the emulator opens up a fullscreen window and 
      scales those graphics to fit it. It will remove the toolbar and then only
      shows the render data in the window. If you want to revert back to window
      mode please use the Hotkey (toggles on/off) : @{b}lalt+enter@{ub}


    @{b}· Screenshot :@{ub}

      By pressing this button you will get a screenshot of what is currently 
      rendered in the main window. A saved image gets stored in @{b}RAM:@{ub} 
      named @{b}MorphUAE-Screenshot.png@{ub}, by default.


    @{b}· Pause/Resume :@{ub}

      This button will Pause or Resume emulation depending on what the current 
      state is. Paused emulation is shown by the window title changing to: 
      @{b}MorphUAE - Paused@{ub}


    @{b}· About :@{ub}

      This button will open the About window...


    @{b}· Joystick port 0 & 1 Selector:@{ub}

      Here you can change the emulated joystick port during the runtime of the 
      emulation. See it as if you virtually swapped out the mouse with a 
      joystick or vice versa.


    @{b}· Save/Load Savestate :@{ub}

      Pressing this you will get a popup menu which lets you to either load or
      save the current machine state. This is great to have incase you need to
      quit your game at the moment but can continue later on.


    @{b}· Eject Disk Images :@{ub}

      Pressing this ejects all previously added/mounted disk images in DF0-DF3


    @{b}· Disk Images / LED indicators :@{ub}
    
      These buttons represent the disk images and will indicate read/write 
      operations when it's active.
      They are enumerated from left to right : DF0, DF1, DF2, DF3.
      Pressing any one of them will open up an ASL-requester to allow you to 
      select the .ADF-file you want to mount/use in the emulator.

      If you drag'n'drop ADF-files into the main window, the emulator will
      mount them into the disk containers (maximum of 4).  
      This allows you to quickly swap disks.

@ENDNODE

@NODE "usage-settings" "MorphUAE - Settings"
@PREV "usage-main"
@NEXT "usage-shell"

 @{b}Settings :@{ub}

   This window consists of a number of tabs that allow you to configure General
   settings for the emulator or specific ones related to the machine type.
   
   All of the configurable tabs have a "Reset to Default" option button - in 
   case you want to reset to the original state of the settings.

    @{b}· General tab :@{ub}

      The @{b}Machine Type@{ub} button will let you select which of the tabs 
      you want to be the default emulated machine when it starts up.
      Each of the selected tabs have options to select specific Kickstart ROM 
      and other machine specific parameters.
      This selection can be overridden by using CLI/Shell flags if needed.

     @{b} Sound section@{ub} will let you configure the output quality of the 
      emulated sound or even disable it completely.

     @{b} I/O Devices@{ub} lets you select which type of emulated device that 
      you will connect to the Mouse/Joystick port. Here you can also set the 
      emulated floppy drive speed. Some games/demos may have issues with faster
      speed higher than normal, so it may be good to try it if issues do occur.

     @{b} Graphics section@{ub} Here you can alter how precisely the emulated 
      graphics should be displayed. If your machine struggles with graphics 
      intensive parts that result in choppy sound or general slugginess, this 
      may be a good "playground" to "fine-tune" it for better results.



    @{b}· OCS tab :@{ub}

      This machine type is intended to emulate the first generations of Amiga 
      with OCS chipset, lower memory footprint and Kickstart 1.x ROM.
      Typical machine here is A500/A1000/A2000 (MC68000)


    @{b}· ECS tab :@{ub}

      This machine type is very similar to the OCS settings but configuration 
      utilises the ECS chipset (and where you can select if you want ECS Agnus, 
      Denise or both emulated. Kickstart 2.x ROM.
      Typical machine A500+/A600 (MC68000).


    @{b}· AGA tab :@{ub}

      This is the last generation of Amigas that use the AGA chipset and default
      2Mb CHIP RAM with optional 1-8 Mb FAST RAM. Kickstart 3.x ROM.
      Typical machine A1200. (MC68020)


    @{b}· Custom tab :@{ub}

      In this machine type you can pretty much customise your emulated machine
      to be whatever you like. You can add up to 2 virtual harddisks (a path to
      your host OS where files can be easily copied from and to). Device and
      Volume name are utilised in the emulated environment. You can select which
      emulated CPU is going to be used (68020, 68040 or 68060).
      There is also an option to turn off the PPC JIT Compiler with a variety of 
      cache-sizes, this option can cause issues during emulation but also boost 
      emulation speeds a huge amount. This is very much dependant of what type
      of binaries/software that are used during emulation.
    

   @{b}· About tab :@{ub}

     Here you can see the software version/revision and credits.


   @{b}· Use, Save or Cancel buttons :@{ub}

     @{b}Use@{ub} button stores all settings in ENV: (Not Saved on QUIT/EXIT)
         restarts emulator USING new settings.

     @{b}Save & Use@{ub} button SAVES all settings in both ENV and ENVARC 
                restarts emulator using new settings.

     @{b}Save@{ub} button SAVES the settings in ENVARC: (Continues as START-UP)
          SAVED settings used at next system reboot.

     @{b}Cancel@{ub} button just closes the settings window.


@ENDNODE

@NODE "usage-shell" "MorphUAE - CLI / Shell"
@PREV "usage-settings"
@NEXT "usage-ambient"

 @{b}CLI/Shell :@{ub}

    @{b}-ocs@{ub} : Overrides default settings and uses those setup under the 
           @{b}OCS tab@{ub} in the settings GUI.

    @{b}-ecs@{ub} : Overrides default settings and uses the ones set in 
           @{b}ECS tab@{ub} in the settings GUI.

    @{b}-aga@{ub} : Overrides default settings and uses those set in 
           @{b}AGA tab@{ub} in the settings GUI.

    @{b}-custom@{ub} : Overrides default settings and uses those set in 
              @{b}Custom tab@{ub} in the settings GUI.

    @{b}-nochecksum@{ub} : Ignores the checksum control of a Kickstart/ROM file
                  some homebrew ROM's don't work, basically it's like a 
                  diagnostic ROM.

    @{b}-fullscreen@{ub} : This will start the emulator in fullscreen mode.

    @{b}-overscan@{ub} : Uses overscan resolution for the display.

    @{b}-0 <ADF file>@{ub} : Insert an ADF disk image on DF0: at startup.
    @{b}-1 <ADF file>@{ub} : Insert an ADF disk image on DF1: at startup.
    @{b}-2 <ADF file>@{ub} : Insert an ADF disk image on DF2: at startup.
    @{b}-3 <ADF file>@{ub} : Insert an ADF disk image on DF3: at startup.

@ENDNODE

@NODE "usage-tooltype" "MorphUAE - Tooltypes"
@PREV "usage-shell"
@NEXT "usage-ambient"

 @{b}Tooltypes :@{ub}

    @{b}OCS@{ub} : Will override the default settings and use what is setup
          under the @{b}OCS tab@{ub} in the settings window.

    @{b}ECS@{ub} : Will override the default settings and use what is setup
          under the @{b}ECS tab@{ub} in the settings window.

    @{b}AGA@{ub} : Will override the default settings and use what is setup
          under the @{b}AGA tab@{ub} in the settings window.

    @{b}CUSTOM@{ub} : Will override the default settings and use what is setup 
             under the @{b}Custom tab@{ub} in the settings window.

    @{b}FULLSCREEN@{ub} : This will start the emulator in fullscreen mode.

    @{b}OVERSCAN@{ub} : Uses overscan resolution for the display.

    @{b}DOUBLEBUFFER@{ub} : Uses doublebuffer on the rendered surface.

@ENDNODE

@NODE "usage-ambient" "MorphUAE - Ambient"
@PREV "usage-tooltype"
@NEXT "usage-hotkeys"

 @{b}Ambient :@{ub}

   If you want to automatically start and load an ADF disk image in Ambient on
   MorphOS you can go to the Settings menu in Ambient.

   Settings
      |
      +- Ambient...

   In the Ambient Settings GUI, locate the "Mime" option in the left panel. Use the
   Search option for 'adf' in the GUI to find "x-adf" type, double-click on it.

   · Add a new event type, name it something appropriate.
   · Change the type to @{b}AmigaDOS@{ub}
   · Set the event type to : DoubleClick
   · Check the option "Change working directory to destination"
   
   · Add the following string to the associated field : 
   
   @{b}run >NIL: Work:Emulators/MorphUAE/MorphUAE -0 %sp@{ub}
   
   You may have to change the above string to suit where MorphUAE is stored.
   ( this is just an example )

@ENDNODE

@NODE "usage-hotkeys" "MorphUAE - Hotkeys"
@PREV "usage-ambient"
@NEXT "usage-arexx"

 @{b}· Toolbar Hotkeys :@{ub}

    · Reset : @{b}ctrl+lalt+r@{ub}
    · Fullscreen/Window toggle : @{b}lalt+enter@{ub}
    · Screenshoot : @{b}ctrl+lalt+s@{ub}
    · Pause/Resume : @{b}ctrl+lalt+p@{ub}
    · Eject Disk Images : @{b}ctrl+lalt+e@{ub}
    · Insert DF0 Image : @{b}ctrl+lalt+1@{ub}
    · Insert DF1 Image : @{b}ctrl+lalt+2@{ub}
    · Insert DF2 Image : @{b}ctrl+lalt+3@{ub}
    · Insert DF3 Image : @{b}ctrl+lalt+4@{ub}

 @{b}· Other Hotkeys :@{ub}

    · Toggle the toolbar on / off : @{b}ctrl+lalt+t@{ub}
    · Quit the emulator : @{b}ctrl+lalt+q@{ub}

@ENDNODE

@NODE "usage-arexx" "MorphUAE - ARexx"
@PREV "usage-hotkeys"
